00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:26.039
It’s happened countless times before: A
thriving American company enters China with

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:29.410
1.4 billion dollar signs in its eyes.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:34.460
It sees in the country almost the ideal market:
A middle class well over twice as large as

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:39.100
its own and without the generational brand
loyalties of America.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:45.010
Because China was relatively late in its technological
development, it almost entirely skipped transitional,

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:50.140
“in-between” inventions like desktop computers
and credit cards, making Chinese consumers

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:54.740
generally less skeptical of new technologies
like smartphones.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:59.990
Best of all, all this is available through
one international expansion, one regulatory

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:03.050
system, one language, and one culture.

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:05.850
Or so, companies think.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.740
What they find is often very different.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:14.500
Relaxed regulation turns out to mean the law
is ambiguous and enforced selectively.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.380
Once a business model is proven, competition
may spring out of nowhere, sometimes with

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.650
no regard for intellectual property.

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:26.979
And what looks like a single, unified market
from the outside becomes thousands of unique

00:01:26.979 --> 00:01:28.390
ones up close.

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:35.119
It’s happened, in one form or another, to
Amazon, Google, Mattel, eBay, Home Depot,

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:37.070
Groupon, and many others.

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:40.410
Uber, however, was sure it was different.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:44.820
It wasn’t ignorant of the titans that had
failed before it, but, in the mind of its

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.861
outspoken founder, Travis Kalanick, Uber had
faced many “impossible challenges” from

00:01:49.861 --> 00:01:54.830
the very beginning — fighting hundreds of
angry cities as the company rapidly expanded

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:56.689
across the U.S.

00:01:56.689 --> 00:02:02.149
Its very business model was at best legally
ambiguous, and yet, it succeeded anyway, giving

00:02:02.149 --> 00:02:07.110
Kalanick a certain unchecked confidence which
made China look like only another of its familiar

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.110
hurdles.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:13.720
And to its credit, Uber did not make the classic
mistake: Directing the expansion from conference

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.140
rooms in San Francisco.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:21.400
It tried very hard to build “Uber China”,
not merely Uber in China.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.520
After boldly declaring it the company’s
“number one priority”, Kalanick devoted

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:28.569
himself to the expansion like nowhere else.

00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:34.470
He spent 70 days of 2015 in the country — nearly
1/5th of the entire year.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.950
He even joked that he should apply for Chinese
citizenship.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:43.230
Uber created a separate, Chinese company,
partnered with local investors like the Chinese

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:49.660
tech giant Baidu, and launched in 2013 in
the country’s largest city, Shanghai.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:52.180
At first, things were rocky.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:57.209
For example, it launched with only US credit
card support, making sure the vast majority

00:02:57.209 --> 00:03:02.110
of locals, who use WeChat and AliPay, were
unable to book rides.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:07.379
Just as embarrassing, the app used Google
Maps, which is notoriously bad in China.

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:13.460
By far Uber’s biggest problem, however,
was, well, its entire business model.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:18.620
The company doesn’t own its cars or hire
its employees, meaning its only value is connecting

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:22.760
people who want to drive to nearby people
to want to ride.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:27.110
But because drivers and riders are regional,
entering a new city is like starting from

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:28.310
scratch.

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:34.060
Uber may have a monopoly in LA, but that won’t
mean anything when it expands to Seattle.

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:38.910
The good thing is that once it has the critical
number of both drivers and riders in a particular

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.610
city, they’re very likely to stick with
Uber.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:46.500
It only takes 2.7 rides, according to the
company, before someone becomes a permanent

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:47.500
customer.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:52.000
Uber’s strategy, therefore, was to grow
with lightning speed.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.819
Every major city had its own general manager,
who would tempt new drivers with bonuses and

00:03:56.819 --> 00:03:59.370
new customers with free rides.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:05.070
Becoming a driver was easy, no long background
checks or complicated forms required.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:06.819
This worked pretty well.

00:04:06.819 --> 00:04:08.299
When it was first.

00:04:08.299 --> 00:04:11.140
The problem was that, in China, it wasn’t.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:16.970
It’s Chinese competitor — Didi — was
founded in 2012 and had everything Uber needed:

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:21.519
immense scale, a China-first design, and the
support of government.

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:26.630
While Uber operated by disrupting the taxi
monopoly in each new city it entered, Didi

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:32.000
was much more old fashioned — it merely
connected riders to existing licensed taxi

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.000
drivers.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:38.580
So instead of inciting chaos and even violence
like Uber, Didi was on the good side of authorities

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:43.690
— even helping manage over 1,300 traffic
lights in partnership with city governments.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:50.850
In 2015, Didi merged with its closest competitor,
giving it near-monopoly control of the market.

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:56.210
It became the only company in the world backed
by all three of China’s tech giants: Baidu,

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:58.540
Alibaba, and Tencent.

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:02.949
Apple and China’s sovereign wealth fund
also invested around that time.

00:05:02.949 --> 00:05:05.930
Uber was behind from day 1.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:11.150
If a Chinese user already had Didi, the only
reason they’d switch is if it was that much

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:12.150
cheaper.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:15.660
To catch up, Uber had to dump insane amounts
of money.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:17.770
And, that’s exactly what it did.

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:24.780
Uber spent a jaw-dropping $40-50 million US
dollars per week on free rides and bonuses

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:25.990
in China.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:30.110
It lost, in total, $1 billion every year.

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:35.740
With Didi, it played a massive game of chicken
— spending this much money was unsustainable,

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:38.289
but who would give up first?

00:05:38.289 --> 00:05:43.680
This metaphorical arms race also created a
kind-of cold war dynamic.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:48.539
Didi allegedly sent undercover engineers to
be hired by Uber, where they would collect

00:05:48.539 --> 00:05:51.310
trade secrets and even conduct sabotage.

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:55.050
On occasion, Uber would be blocked from WeChat.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:59.420
In China, this was the equivalent of Google
hiding a competitor from its search results

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:03.350
— making it effectively invisible to the
vast majority of the country.

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:09.580
And besides burning through cash like it was
fuel, this subsidy-war also led to an unintended

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:11.990
side-effect: fraud.

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:16.889
In one Chinese city, Uber reported having
as many drivers as London, Paris, and San

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:18.699
Francisco combined.

00:06:18.699 --> 00:06:21.110
But how many of them were real?

00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:26.020
When a “new” user was offered a free promotional
Uber ride, the company still paid the driver

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:27.430
as normal.

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:28.960
This created a loophole.

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:33.919
Two users, one acting as the “driver”,
and the other as the “rider” could collude,

00:06:33.919 --> 00:06:36.830
taking a fake ride, and splitting the profit.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:42.400
Soon, scammers created entire circuit boards
with rows of SIM card slots, each simulating

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:43.940
a fake phone.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:48.100
They would take a fake ride, swap out SIM
cards, and repeat.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:52.889
In one Chinese city, fraud accounted for an
estimated half of all rides.

00:06:52.889 --> 00:06:59.590
Over 30,000 fake rides were taken every day
in the summer of 2015, just in China.

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:04.730
Uber fought back by creating a database of
IMEI numbers, which are unique to a given

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:09.479
phone and thus allowed the company to identify
a scammer even after they had reset their

00:07:09.479 --> 00:07:10.479
phone.

00:07:10.479 --> 00:07:16.770
However, Apple hid this number from developers
starting in 2012 for its users’ privacy,

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:19.139
preventing Uber from detecting fraud.

00:07:19.139 --> 00:07:24.900
Uber was undeterred and hired a 3rd party
hacking company to bypass iOS’ security

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:29.320
rules, which, Apple discovered, warning them
to stop.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:34.210
In its typical fashion, Uber continued anyway,
adding a line of code to check if the app

00:07:34.210 --> 00:07:39.160
was running in Cupertino, California, where
Apple is headquartered, and if so, not break

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:40.410
its rules.

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:44.780
Apple still found out, and called a very angry
meeting.

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:47.330
Things were not going well for Uber.

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:53.650
It faced massive fraud, trouble with Apple,
and over $1 billion a year in losses.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:56.780
Then came the final nail in the coffin.

00:07:56.780 --> 00:08:01.789
For its first few years in China, Uber operated
in a legal grey area.

00:08:01.789 --> 00:08:06.740
Like in the U.S., this subjected it to varying
levels of retribution based on the mood of

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:08.800
each municipal government.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:13.760
In the Southern city of Guangzhou, police
raided the Uber office at midnight, accusing

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:15.789
it of running an illegal business.

00:08:15.789 --> 00:08:21.190
In Hangzhou, another police raid was followed
by a violent confrontation between Uber and

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:22.840
taxi drivers.

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:30.379
All this changed in July 2016, after a series
of high-profile crimes committed by Didi drivers.

00:08:30.379 --> 00:08:34.909
China legalized the industry, which came with
strict new regulation, like there could be

00:08:34.909 --> 00:08:39.350
no subsidies and drivers required 3-years
of experience.

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:44.450
From now on, Uber would need to request formal
approval from the local and national government

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:47.100
before expanding to each new city.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:53.140
At the beginning of that year, Uber operated
in 37 Chinese cities to Didi’s 400, and

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:58.550
had 229 million daily active users compared
with 908.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:03.430
Just a few short years after it had sprinted
into the country with supreme confidence,

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:06.940
it had all finally become too much.

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:11.899
Just days after the new regulations were passed,
Uber China was sold to Didi.

00:09:11.899 --> 00:09:17.790
Uber took a 19% stake in Didi, and the leaders
of both received positions on each other’s

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:19.350
board of directors.

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:24.450
Whether this was an embarrassing failure or
a diligent strategic decision is still open

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:25.850
for debate.

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:31.500
On one hand, Uber unequivocally did not achieve
its original goals of conquering the Chinese

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:32.500
market.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:36.040
It left with only a minuscule market share
and incredible losses.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:41.279
It did not succeed where others had failed
in China, and even though government protectionism

00:09:41.279 --> 00:09:47.100
may be somewhat to blame, Uber certainly made
many unforced errors along the way.

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:52.610
On the other hand, Uber spent $2 billion on
China and left with assets valued at seven

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:54.250
billion dollars.

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:58.959
Viewed purely from the balance sheet, China
was, unlike most of the company’s markets,

00:09:58.959 --> 00:10:01.330
a source of pure profit.

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:06.810
It could now invest that money in other, more
win-able markets in Southeast Asia and elsewhere.

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:09.190
So, a failure, yes.

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:12.320
But, also, a profitable one.

